L000201	    \email{martin.plenio@uni-ulm.de}
L000202		\affiliation{\ulm}
L000203		\author{Nelly H.Y. Ng}
L000204	    \email{nelly.hy.ng@gmail.com}
L000205		\affiliation{\ntu}
L000206		\affiliation{\cqt}
L000207		\author{Jeongrak Son}
L000208		\email{jeongrak.son@gmail.com}
L000209		\affiliation{\ntu}
L000210	    \affiliation{Dahlem Center for Complex Quantum Systems, Freie Universität Berlin, 14195 Berlin, Germany}
L000211		\begin{abstract}
L000212	    Time-translation symmetry strongly constrains physical dynamics, yet systematic characterization for continuous-variable systems lags behind its discrete-variable counterpart. We close this gap by providing a rigorous classification of Gaussian quantum operations that are covariant under time translations, termed Gaussian covariant operations. We show that several key results known for discrete-variable covariant operations break down in the Gaussian optical setting: discrepancies arise in physical and thermodynamic implementation, in the extensivity of asymmetry, and in catalytic advantages. Our results provide comprehensive mathematical and operational toolkits for Gaussian covariant operations, including a peculiar pair of asymmetry measures that are completely non‑extensive. Our findings also reveal surprising consequences of the interplay among symmetry, Gaussianity, and thermodynamic constraints, suggesting that real-world scenarios with multiple constraints have a rich structure not accessible from examining individual constraints separately.
L000213		\end{abstract}
L000214		\maketitle
L000215	
L000216	Time flows whether we have a clock or not.
L000217	Without a clock, however, we cannot keep track of temporal information, which severely limits our ability to control the state.
L000218	Suppose that a clock-less agent applies a quantum channel to a system that is prepared in a known initial state, but the agent does not know how much time has passed since preparation.
L000219	Then any information the agent has about the system after the operation must be averaged over all possible timings of the channel.
L000220	Mathematically, this corresponds to time-averaged versions of channels, which form a class of $\mathrm{U}(1)$-covariant operations, hereafter referred to as phase-covariant operations~\cite{Bartlett07}.
L000221	
L000222	To access the full information, timekeeping devices such as clocks are needed.
L000223	Then the operations before the time-averaging, which are not covariant in general, can be considered.
L000224	A common way to model such processes is to treat the timekeeping device as an additional quantum system and describe the overall evolution on the enlarged system by a globally covariant operation. Notably, timekeeping devices generally degrade with use unless additional resources are supplied~\cite{Erker2017Clock, Woods2019_Clocks}.
L000225	Thus, to study the cost of these devices, a good understanding of covariant operations is crucial.
L000226	
L000227	Quantum resource theories~\cite{Gour25} are developed precisely for such careful accounting of resources.
L000228	The properties of covariant operations and how they utilize timekeeping devices are extensively studied in the context of the resource theory of asymmetry~\cite{Bartlett07, Gour08, MarvianThesis}.
L000229	This approach has proved useful for understanding quantum metrology~\cite{Streltsov2017_Coherence, Marvian2014_Noether}, quantum clocks and reference frames~\cite{Bartlett07}, and the cost of realizing quantum measurements~\cite{Tajima22}.
L000230	Most resource-theoretic studies to date have focused on finite-dimensional systems, whilst time-translation symmetry for continuous-variable systems, especially Gaussian optical ones, has been studied separately under the name phase-insensitive operators.
L000231	In particular, seminal results, such as studies on linear amplifiers~\cite{Haus1962, Caves1982, Clerk2010Amp}, the resolution of the minimum output entropy conjecture for Gaussian bosonic channels~\cite{Giovannetti04, Giovannetti13, DePalma14, DePalma15}, extensive studies of the classical and quantum capacities of such channels~\cite{Holevo01, Giovannetti04Cap}, and analyses of boson sampling~\cite{Aaronson10, Hamilton17}, have relied heavily on time-translation symmetry.
L000232	Yet, Gaussian operations that are time-translation symmetric---which we call Gaussian covariant operations in the rest of this letter---have rarely been studied in their full generality.
L000233	
L000234	In this work, we unify and generalize existing studies on Gaussian phase-insensitive operations into a framework that encompasses input and output systems with arbitrary numbers of modes.
L000235	In particular, we establish both mathematical and physical characterizations of Gaussian covariant operations, namely i) a necessary and sufficient condition for them to be freely dilatable and ii) a monotone function that does not increase under Gaussian covariant operations and exhibits several peculiar properties.
L000236	Each of these results showcases surprising deviations from general time-translation symmetric operations and from Gaussian operations when both conditions are imposed.
L000237	
L000238	Lastly, we apply our characterization results to thermodynamics, where time-translation symmetry plays a central role~\cite{Lostaglio15, Korzekwa2016, Smirne16, Kwon2018, Shiraishi2025GPC}.
L000239	Since time-translation-covariant unitaries coincide with energy-preserving unitaries, demanding the first law (total energy preservation) amounts to requiring operations to be time-translation covariant unless free coherence is supplied via auxiliary systems.
L000240	Moreover, a version of the second law can be encoded as Gibbs‑state preservation (non-increasing non‑equilibrium free energies).
L000241	Enhanced thermal operations~\cite{EnTO2015} are defined as the set of all operations satisfying these two conditions.
L000242	However, not all enhanced thermal operations are freely implementable: there exists a gap~\cite{Ding21} between them and the set of operations realizable with a Gibbs-state ancilla and an energy-preserving unitary, known as thermal operations~\cite{Janzing00_TO}.
L000243	Understanding the exact nature of this gap (beyond its origin in asymmetry manipulation) remains a long-standing open problem~\cite{Lostaglio15Symmetry, Gour2018EnTO, Lie2025TO}; progress here will elucidate the genuinely quantum aspects of thermodynamic operations.
L000244	In Gaussian optics, time-translation-covariant unitaries correspond exactly to passive elements~\cite{SerafiniBook, Weedbrook12}, and the Gaussian analogue of thermal operations has been introduced~\cite{Serafini20, Narasimhachar2021GTO, Yadin22}.
L000245	We show that, in this setting, the gap closes: Gaussian thermal operations and Gaussian enhanced thermal operations coincide, resolving the question for a large and experimentally relevant class.
L000246	
L000247	\emph{Notations}---
L000248	We consider continuous-variable systems consisting of $m$ bosonic modes with Hamiltonian $H=\sum_{j=1}^m\omega_j\hat{a}_j^\dagger \hat{a}_j$, where $\hat a_j$ and $\hat a_j^\dagger$ are the annihilation/creation operators and $\omega_j> 0$ is the frequency of the $j$th mode.
L000249	A Gaussian state of this system is fully characterized by its first and second moments defined as $r_{j} = r_{m+j}^{*} = \langle \hat{a}_{j} \rangle$ for $j = 1,\cdots,m$ and $M_{j,k} = M_{k+m,j+m}= \frac{1}{2}\langle \{\hat{a}_{j},\hat{a}_{k}^{\dagger}\} \rangle - \langle \hat{a}_{j}\rangle \langle \hat{a}_{k}^{\dagger}\rangle $, $M_{j,k+m} = M_{j+m,k}^{*} = \frac{1}{2}\langle \{\hat{a}_{j},\hat{a}_{k}\} \rangle - \langle \hat{a}_{j}\rangle \langle \hat{a}_{k}\rangle $ for $j,k = 1,\cdots,m$, respectively.
L000250	Accordingly, the first moment vector $\vecr$ and the second moment matrix $M$ can be written as
L000251	\begin{align}\label{eq:cvm}
L000252	    \vecr=\begin{pmatrix}
L000253	        \vecalpha^* \\ \vecalpha
L000254	    \end{pmatrix},\ M = \begin{pmatrix}
L000255	        \mu^* & \chi \\ \chi^* & \mu
L000256	    \end{pmatrix},
L000257	\end{align}
L000258	where $\vecalpha$ is a $m$-dimensional vector and $\mu$ and $\chi$ are $m\times m$-dimensional matrices that are Hermitian and symmetric, respectively.
L000259	Hence, a Gaussian state is fully characterized by $(\vec\alpha,\mu,\chi)$.
L000260	Moreover, the second moment must obey the uncertainty principle, which requires $M+\mathbf{Z}\geq \mathbf{0}$. Here $\mathbf{Z}\coloneqq \frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix}
L000261		\mathbb{I} & \mathbf{0}\\ \mathbf{0} & -\mathbb{I}
L000262	\end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbb{I}$ is the $m\times m$ identity matrix.
L000263	Note that we use the first and the second moments defined with $\hat{a}_j$, $\hat{a}_j^\dagger$ operators instead of the more popular choice of $\hat{x}_{j} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\hat{a}_{j}^{\dagger}+\hat{a}_{j})$, $\hat{p}_{j} = \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}(\hat{a}_{j}^{\dagger} - \hat{a}_{j})$; these two representations are equivalent up to a simple transformation and we choose the former for notational simplicity in most of our proofs.
L000264	
L000265	Similarly, Gaussian operations are specified by the tuple $(\tX, \tY,\td)$ acting on the first and second moments as $\vecr \rightarrow \tX \vecr + \td$ and $M\rightarrow \tX M \tX^\dagger + \tY$, with the constraint $\tY \geq \tX \mathbf{Z} \tX^\dagger - \mathbf{Z}$.
L000266	We also allow scenarios where the input and the output modes are different.
L000267	These operations emerge when e.g. input system $S_\mathrm{in}$ interact with ancilla system $S_\mathrm{out}R$ followed by the partial trace of $S_\mathrm{in}R$.
L000268	In such cases, $\tX$ may not be square matrices, and the constraint becomes $\tY \geq \tX \mathbf{Z}_\mathrm{in} \tX^\dagger - \mathbf{Z}_\mathrm{out}$.
L000269	For Gaussian unitary operations, the tuple simplifies to $(\tS, 0,\td)$, where $\td$ describes the displacement operation, and $\tS$ (satisfying $\tS\mmZ \tS^\dagger=\mmZ$) is the total effect from beam-splitters, phase-shifters and squeezing.
L000270	
L000271	\emph{Mathematical Characterization}---
L000272	In this work, we are mainly interested in operations that are covariant to time-translation under the free Hamiltonian.
L000273	A \emph{Gaussian covariant operation (GCO)} can be formally defined as a Gaussian channel $\mE_{\mathrm{GCO}}$ that commutes with time-translation:
L000274	\begin{align}\label{eq:GCO_condition}
L000275	    \mE_{\mathrm{GCO}}\circ\mathcal{U}_t^\mathrm{in}=\mathcal{U}_t^\mathrm{out}\circ\mE_{\mathrm{GCO}}
L000276	\end{align}
L000277	for all $t\in\mathbb{R}$, where $\mathcal{U}_t^\mathrm{in/out}(\cdot)=e^{-iH_\mathrm{in/out}t}(\cdot)e^{iH_\mathrm{in/out}t}$, and $H_\mathrm{in/out}$ are the free Hamiltonians of the input and output systems of $\mE_{\mathrm{GCO}}$.
L000278	
L000279	The commutation relation Eq.~\eqref{eq:GCO_condition} greatly simplifies the structure of GCO.
L000280	Proposition~\ref{pro: indep evols} in Appendix~\ref{ap:proofs1} demonstrates that input modes $j,k$ of different frequencies $\omega_{j}\neq\omega_{k}$ evolve independently under GCO.
L000281	It is therefore sufficient to study the case where all input and output modes have the same frequency $\omega$.
L000282	Hence, the time-translation operation $\mathcal{U}_{t}$ is equivalent to an overall phase shift by $\theta = \omega t$, corresponding to a tuple $(\tR(\theta),0,0)$, where $\tR(\theta) = \cos(\theta) \mathbb{I}-i\sin(\theta) \cdot 2\mathbf{Z}$.
L000283	
L000284	The mathematical characterization of GCOs is derived in the literature~\cite{Heinosaari2010GaugeCov, Holevo2015GaugeCov, Frank2017GaugeCov} with the name Gaussian gauge-covariant operations.
L000285	In our notation, the characterization can be stated as follows.
L000286	Any $\Inm$-mode to $\Outm$-mode GCO is \emph{fully characterized} by a pair $(A,B)$ of $\Outm\times \Inm$ matrix $A$ and $\Outm\times \Outm$ matrix $B$, transforming first and second moments as:
L000287	    \begin{align}
L000288	        \vecalpha &\rightarrow A\vecalpha,\label{eq:1st_moment}\\
L000289	        \mu & \rightarrow   A\mu A^\dagger + B,\label{eq:2nd_moment_mu}\\
L000290	        \chi &\rightarrow A^* \chi A^\dagger,\label{eq:2nd_moment_chi}
L000291	    \end{align}
L000292	    with the constraint
L000293	    \begin{align}
L000294	    	B\geq \pm\frac{1}{2}(  \iden-AA^\dagger).\label{eq:HUR_A_B}
L000295		\end{align}
L000296	For completeness, we provide a self-contained proof in Lemma~\ref{le:gene_GCO_app}, Appendix~\ref{ap:proofs1}.
L000297	This characterization greatly simplifies the analysis of GCO by reducing the tuple $(X,Y,\td)$ to the much smaller pair $(A,B)$ and showing that second moments $\mu$ and $\chi$ evolve independently.
L000298	Furthermore, if a GCO $(A,B)$ is unitary, corresponding to passive elements in optics or energy-preserving unitaries in thermodynamics, the reversibility of unitary operations demands $B=0$, and in turn, $AA^\dagger=\iden$ from Eq.~\eqref{eq:HUR_A_B}.
L000299	Therefore, a unitary GCO is fully characterized by a single $m\times m$ unitary matrix $A=V$, which acts on the first and second moments as $\vecalpha\rightarrow V\vecalpha$, $\mu\rightarrow V\mu V^\dagger$, and $\chi \rightarrow V^* \chi V^\dagger$.
L000300	
L000301	\emph{Physical and operational characterization}---
L000302	Beyond the mathematical characterization, we provide operational ones.
L000303	Typically, a desired quantum channel is implemented by its dilation, i.e., by preparing an auxiliary state and applying a unitary.
L000304	For this dilation to be free, the auxiliary and the unitary both need to be free.
L000305	We define a class of GCOs with such free dilations (also known as physically implementable operations in the literature~\cite{Gour25}).
L000306	\begin{definition}[Freely dilatable GCO]\label{def: free dilation}
L000307		A GCO $\mE$ on system $S$ has a \emph{free dilation}, if it can be written as
L000308		\begin{align}\label{eq: PI GCO def}
L000309			\mE(\cdot) = \tr_R\left[\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}}(\cdot\otimes\rho_{R})\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}\dagger}\right],
L000310		\end{align}
L000311	where $\rho_{R}$ is a Gaussian symmetric state satisfying $[\rho_{R},H_{R}] = 0$
L000312	for the Hamiltonian $H_{R}$, and $\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}}$ is a Gaussian covariant unitary on $SR$, i.e., $[\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}},H_S+H_R]=0$.
L000313	\end{definition}
L000314	
L000315	Here symmetric states are those invariant under time-translation, or equivalently, under phase-shifts.
L000316	In our $(\vec{\alpha},\mu,\chi)$ notation, this corresponds to $\vec{\alpha} = 0$ and $\chi = 0$.
L000317	In the following lemma (proved in Lemma~\ref{le:free_dilation_app}, Appendix~\ref{ap:proofs1_2}), we provide a complete characterization of the GCOs that admit a free dilation.
L000318	\begin{lemma}\label{le:free_dilation}
L000319	A GCO $(A,B)$ is freely dilatable if and only if the following two conditions are satisfied.\\
L000320	(F1) $\iden-AA^\dagger\geq0$;\\
L000321	(F2) $\supp(B)= \supp(\iden-AA^\dagger)$.
L000322	\end{lemma}
L000323	
L000324	Using Lemma~\ref{le:free_dilation}, we also identify an operationally relevant class of GCOs that do not admit a free dilation in Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI} (proved in Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI_app}, Appendix~\ref{ap:proofs1_2}).
L000325	
L000326	\begin{theorem}
L000327	\label{th:GCO_PI}
L000328	    If a GCO mapping a system to itself has no fixed-point that is a valid quantum state, then it is not freely dilatable.
L000329	    However, the converse is not true.
L000330	\end{theorem}
L000331	Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI} is a concrete instance of Lemma~\ref{le:free_dilation} and provides the physical intuition behind the impossibility of a free implementation.
L000332	We present this intuition here, in summary, and in Appendix~\ref{ap:proofs1_2}, in detail.
L000333	If a GCO (from a system to itself) has no fixed-point quantum state, it must be capable of increasing the energy of the system’s hottest mode, irrespective of how large that energy already is.
L000334	Conversely, if a GCO is freely dilatable, it is implemented by a Gaussian covariant unitary $\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}}$ as in Eq.~\eqref{eq: PI GCO def}, and Gaussian covariant unitaries (i.e. beam splitters and phase shifters) cannot further excite the hottest mode.
L000335	Consequently, when the system’s hottest mode is initially hotter than any mode of the ancilla, its energy cannot increase under a freely dilatable GCO.
L000336	This contradicts the required energy‑increase of a GCO without a fixed point.
L000337	
L000338	Lemma~\ref{le:free_dilation} and Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI} reveal that not all GCOs admit a free dilation.
L000339	This is not the case for general time-translation covariant operations, where any covariant map (including infinite-dimensional ones) can be dilated using a symmetric auxiliary and a covariant unitary~\cite{Keyl99, MarvianThesis}.
L000340	Nor is it the case for general Gaussian operations, which all admit a dilation with a Gaussian auxiliary and a Gaussian unitary~\cite{SerafiniBook}.
L000341	These discrepancies follow from the intuition above: covariant unitaries and Gaussian unitaries can further excite the system’s hottest mode, whereas unitaries that are Gaussian \emph{and} covariant cannot.
L000342	
L000343	Lemma~\ref{le:free_dilation} and Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI} also sharply contrast with recent results that every GCO admits a Gaussian-covariant dilation~\cite{Koukoulekidis2025}.
L000344	The main difference lies in the allowed auxiliaries.
L000345	In our framework frequencies are required to be positive; Ref.~\cite{Koukoulekidis2025} allows auxiliary modes with negative frequencies, making the energy spectrum unbounded from below.
L000346	Although unphysical in the Schr\"{o}dinger picture, negative effective frequencies can appear in a rotating (modulation) frame~\cite{PhysRevA.31.3068,PhysRevX.2.031016}.
L000347	In that picture two bosonic modes can have effective frequencies $\pm \omega$ and two-mode squeezing unitaries become covariant under the modulated free Hamiltonian.
L000348	The most salient examples are Gaussian amplifiers~\cite{Holevo2015GaugeCov}.
L000349	They increase the system's energy regardless of the initial state and thus have no fixed-point.
L000350	Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI} then implies that they do not admit a free dilation; however, within the framework of Ref.~\cite{Koukoulekidis2025}, they are freely dilatable.
L000351	The main difference is that a two‑mode‑squeezing unitary---available when negative‑frequency modes are allowed---can further excite the hottest mode, unlike passive linear unitaries.
L000352	In this case, the conserved quantity associated with the time‑translation symmetry is no longer the original free Hamiltonian but the modulated one.
L000353	Consequently, implementations of covariant operations defined in the modulation picture generally require external energy source (e.g. a strong pump beam at high frequency~\cite{PhysRevA.31.3068}) and are therefore not thermodynamically free.
L000354	In contrast, $\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}}$ in Definition~\ref{def: free dilation} is both covariant and energy-preserving, making it more relevant for thermodynamic considerations.
L000355	
L000356	We can attempt to measure the resource cost required for implementing non-freely dilatable (but resource non-generating) GCOs.
L000357	The cost can be measured as the amount of resource that must be supplied in the auxiliary state to implement the desired operation; such a framework is standard in quantifying thermodynamic~\cite{Bennett82, Faist15, Faist18} and asymmetry cost~\cite{Tajima20, Tajima22, Tajima25GP}.
L000358	In the case of GCOs, Lemma~\ref{le:free_dilation_app} (Appendix~\ref{ap:proofs1_2}) shows that any auxiliary state (non‑Gaussian and/or asymmetric) cannot implement a GCO without a free dilation using Gaussian covariant unitaries.
L000359	In other words, the cost of implementing such operations is infinite.
L000360	
L000361	On the other hand, \emph{Gaussian thermal operations (GTO)}~\cite{Serafini20, Narasimhachar2021GTO} constitute an important class of freely dilatable GCOs.
L000362	A GTO is defined as a freely dilatable GCO with the constraint that the ancilla $\rho_{R}$ is a Gibbs state at ambient temperature $\beta^{-1}$, i.e., $\rho_{R} = e^{- \beta H_R}/\tr[{e^{-\beta H_R}}]$.
L000363	It is the Gaussian analogue of thermal operations~\cite{Janzing00_TO} defined as channels implementable with an energy-preserving unitary and a Gibbs state ancilla.
L000364	Thermal operations exactly constitute the set of freely dilatable operations in thermodynamic theories, yet they are notoriously hard to characterize, as are the state transformations resulting from them.
L000365	This motivated the definition of a slightly larger set known as enhanced thermal operations~\cite{EnTO2015}.
L000366	Enhanced thermal operations are defined axiomatically as the set of covariant operations that preserve the Gibbs state at the ambient temperature, and are therefore easier to characterize~\cite{Gour2018EnTO}.
L000367	Unfortunately, the set of enhanced thermal operations is strictly larger than that of thermal operations
L000368	
L000369	Unfortunately, the set of enhanced thermal operations is strictly larger than that of thermal operations at the level of state transformations, i.e. there exists a pair of states that an enhanced thermal operation can map, while no thermal operation can achieve even an approximate version of this transformation~\cite{Ding21}.
L000370	This separation is stronger than the one at the channel level, which only implies that some enhanced thermal operations are not thermal operations and does not rule out the possibility that every state transformation achievable by an enhanced thermal operation could be reproduced by a different thermal operation.
L000371	
L000372	
L000373	The \emph{Gaussian analogue of enhanced thermal operations (GEnTO)} can be defined similarly, as the subset of GCO that preserves a Gibbs state.
L000374	Surprisingly, we show that every GEnTO is freely dilatable using thermodynamically free and Gaussian resources.
L000375	
L000376	\begin{theorem}\label{th:gento_gto}
L000377	A Gaussian enhanced thermal operation is always a Gaussian thermal operation.
L000378	\end{theorem}
L000379	A more technical statement would be the following.
L000380	Let $\mE_{\mathrm{GEnTO}}$ be a GEnTO on system $S$.
L000381	Then there exist an auxiliary Gibbs state $\gamma_R=\frac{e^{-\beta H_R}}{\tr[e^{-\beta H_R}]}$ and a unitary GCO $\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}}$, such that $\mE_{\mathrm{GEnTO}}(\cdot)=\tr_R[\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}}(\cdot\otimes\gamma_R)\mathbf{U}^{\mathrm{PC}\dagger}]$.
L000382	See Theorem~\ref{th:gento_gto_app}, Appendix~\ref{ap: EnTO} for a proof.
L000383	This result contrasts with the scenario without the Gaussian constraint, where a gap exists between thermal operations and enhanced thermal operations at the level of state transformations.
L000384	Theorem~\ref{th:gento_gto} indicates that imposing the Gaussian-preserving constraint on both classes removes this gap and, moreover, establishes a stronger equivalence at the level of channels.
L000385	
L000386	Consider the simplest case of a single-mode GCO $(A,B)$.
L000387	If it has a physical fixed-point $(\vec{\alpha},\mu,\chi)$, it also fixes a Gaussian symmetric state $(0,\mu,0)$, which is a single-mode Gibbs state at temperature $\beta(\mu)=\frac{1}{\omega}\ln\frac{\mu-1/2}{\mu+1/2}$.
L000388	Thus Theorem~\ref{th:gento_gto} implies that any single-mode GCO with a fixed-point $(\vec{\alpha},\mu,\chi)$ is a GTO implemented at temperature $\beta(\mu)$, and consequently, is freely dilatable.
L000389	
L000390	Unlike the single-mode scenario, the existence of a fixed-point is no longer sufficient to guarantee physical implementability for multi-mode systems, as shown in Theorem~\ref{th:GCO_PI}.
L000391	Nevertheless, Theorem~\ref{th:gento_gto} guarantees that if a Gibbs state is one of the fixed-points, a GCO is freely dilatable.
L000392	
L000393	\emph{Completely non-extensive monotones}---
L000394	We have so far focused on the properties of GCO channels themselves; below we analyze second moments transformations $(\mu,\chi)\stackrel{ \mathrm{GCO}}{\longrightarrow}(\mu',\chi')$ induced by these channels.
L000395	For this purpose, we identify a pair of monotones.
L000396	
L000397	\begin{definition}\label{def: Sl monotones}
L000398		Let $(\mu,\chi)$ be the second moment of a quantum state.
L000399		$\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi)$ are defined as
L000400		\begin{align}\label{eq: Sl monotone def}
L000401			\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi) \coloneqq \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{1}\left[ \left(\mu^{*}\pm\frac{\iden}{2}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\chi\ \left(\mu\pm\frac{\iden}{2}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\right],
L000402		\end{align}
L000403		where $(\cdot)^{-\frac{1}{2}}$ denotes the pseudoinverse of $(\cdot)^{\frac{1}{2}}$ and $\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{1}[\cdot]$ denotes the largest singular value.
L000404	\end{definition}
L000405	
L000406	Intuitively, $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi)$ quantify the asymmetry of a state: they increase when $\chi$ becomes more prominent with $\mu$ fixed.
L000407	Yet, they do not capture all the aspects of asymmetry.
L000408	In particular, while quantifying the asymmetry in the second moment (type-2 asymmetry), they are independent of the first moment and thus fail to capture the asymmetry induced by displacements (type-1 asymmetry).
L000409	It has been found that type-1 and type-2 asymmetry are not interconvertible via GCO~\cite{Koukoulekidis2025}.
L000410	Here, we focus solely on type-2 asymmetry.
L000411	
L000412	We now prove several important properties of $\Sl_{\pm}$ functions.
L000413	\begin{itemize}
L000414		\item[(P1)]  Finiteness and faithfulness: $0\leq \Sl_\pm(\mu,\chi)<\infty$ with $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi) = 0$ if and only if $\chi = 0$.
L000415		\item [(P2)] Monotonicity: $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi) \geq \Sl_{\pm}(\mu',\chi')$ whenever $(\mu,\chi)\toGCO(\mu',\chi')$.
L000416		\item [(P3)] Complete non-extensiveness: $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu_{1}\oplus\mu_{2},\chi_{1}\oplus\chi_{2}) = \max\{\Sl_{\pm}(\mu_{1},\chi_{1}),\, \Sl_{\pm}(\mu_{2},\chi_{2})\}$.
L000417	\end{itemize}
L000418	(P1--3) are proved in Proposition~\ref{proposition: kernels inclusion}, Lemmas~\ref{le:monotones_app} and~\ref{le:monotones-composite}, respectively.
L000419	Note that $(\mu_{1}\oplus\mu_{2},\chi_{1}\oplus\chi_{2})$ represents the second moment of a tensor product of two states each with the second moments $(\mu_{1},\chi_{1})$ and $(\mu_{2},\chi_{2})$.
L000420	
L000421	Among the properties, the complete non-extensiveness of (P3) is rare and entails surprising consequences for state transformations.
L000422	Importantly, it rules out any multi-copy or distillation processes achieving higher $\Sl_{\pm}$ values.
L000423	To see this, suppose that $(\oplus_{i=1}^{N}\mu,\oplus_{i=1}^{N}\chi)\toGCO(\oplus_{i=1}^{M}\mu',\oplus_{i=1}^{M}\chi')$.
L000424	(P2) and (P3) imply that $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi) \geq  \Sl_{\pm}(\mu',\chi')$, regardless of $N$ and $M$.
L000425	Therefore, arbitrarily many copies of a state with $(\mu,\chi)$ cannot yield even a single copy of a state with $(\mu',\chi')$ if $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu',\chi')>\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi)$.
L000426	Similar no-go theorems have been established for various tasks when the allowed operations are restricted to be Gaussian, including distillation of entanglement~\cite{Eisert2002_GaussianDistillation} and squeezing~\cite{Lami2018_GaussianQRT}, quantum error correction~\cite{Niset2009_GaussianQEC}, and quantum sensing~\cite{hnhp-jhr2}.
L000427	Yet, our result on asymmetry is especially extreme:
L000428	while the marginal asymptotic distillation rate of asymmetry diverges for almost all initial states in the finite-dimensional (and thus non-Gaussian) case~\cite{Shiraishi2024_ArbitraryAmplification}, the complete non-extensiveness of $\Sl_{\pm}$ here implies that it is impossible to accumulate type-2 asymmetry across independent subsystems when the Gaussian condition is imposed.
L000429	Note that our monotones only forbid the distillation of type-2 asymmetry (concerning second moments) while type-1 asymmetry is known to be distillable via GCOs~\cite{Yadavalli2025}.
L000430	
L000431	Finally, we study whether catalysts can relax state transformation conditions for GCOs.
L000432	Catalysts are auxiliary systems that participate in the process to activate otherwise prohibited transformations~\cite{JonathanP1999, LipkaBartosik2024_CatalysisReview}.
L000433	For instance, even if the direct transformation $\rho_{S}\to\rho'_{S'}$ cannot be performed using a set of allowed operations, a transformation $\rho_{S}\otimes\tau_{C} \to \rho'_{S'}\otimes\tau_{C}$ (strict catalysis) or $\to \varrho_{S'C}$ such that $\Tr_{C}[\varrho_{S'C}] = \rho'_{S}$ and $\Tr_{S'}[\varrho_{S'C}] = \tau_{C}$ (correlated catalysis) is often achievable with an allowed operation.
L000434	In both strict and correlated catalysis, the catalyst state $\tau_{C}$ is (marginally) preserved and can be reused for an indefinite number of times.
L000435	Correlated catalysis, in particular, is found to relax almost all state transformation conditions in various resource theories, including the theory of athermality~\cite{Shiraishi2021_GP,Son2024hierarchy,  Shiraishi2025GPC, shiraishi2025recoverysecondlawfully}, nonuniformity~\cite{Wilming2022correlationsin}, entanglement (for pure states)~\cite{Kondra2021Entanglement}.
L000436	In the case of $\mathrm{U}(1)$-asymmetry, i.e. when covariant operations (not necessarily Gaussian) are allowed, correlated catalysis even enables arbitrary amplification of the asymmetry~\cite{Ding21_Asymmetry, Shiraishi2024_ArbitraryAmplification, Kondra2024_CoherenceAsymmetry}.
L000437	Remarkably, correlated catalysis loses its power of amplifying the type-2 asymmetry when Gaussianity is imposed.
L000438	\begin{itemize}
L000439		\item[(P4)] $\Sl_{\pm}(\mu,\chi)$ are monotonic under (correlated) catalytic transformations.
L000440	\end{itemize}
L000441	A full technical statement and its proof are in Lemma~\ref{le: no catalysis}.
L000442	
L000443	
L000444	
L000445	
L000446	\emph{Discussions}---
L000447	In this work, we studied the interplay between Gaussianity and time-translation covariance, two properties well-studied separately but rarely together.
L000448	Our main technical results are the characterization of Gaussian covariant operations.
L000449	We fully characterized the subset with a free dilation, the structure of operations with a fixed-point, and monotone functions that never increase after an operation.
L000450	We also provide a useful decomposition for operations with an asymmetric fixed-point as Lemma~\ref{le: fixed point decomposition} in Section~\ref{ap:proofs1_2}.
L000451	These characterizations will be useful for future studies on Gaussian covariant operations, which encompass numerous important operations in optics.
L000452	In particular, we expect the new, completely non-extensive monotones to serve as simple and powerful tools for studying general state transformation problems.
L000453	
L000454	The significance of our results is not limited to Gaussian covariant operations.
L000455	By contrasting general time-translation‑symmetric quantum operations and thermal processes with their Gaussian subset, we obtain three main contrasts arising from Gaussianity.
L000456	First, we identify a subset of Gaussian covariant operations that cannot be dilated into any free unitary, indicating a discrepancy between axiomatically defined and operationally defined sets.
L000457	This separation is reminiscent of similar ones that appear when additional restrictions such as Markovianity~\cite{Spaventa2022MTO, Korzekwa2022MTO} or locality~\cite{Marvian22Locality} are imposed.
L000458	Second, we prove that asymptotic or catalytic advantages vanish for single-shot restrictions given by the monotones $\Sl_{\pm}$ introduced here.
L000459	This mirrors the impossibility of Gaussian resource distillation and broadcasting in various setups~\cite{Eisert2002_GaussianDistillation, Lami2018_GaussianQRT, Chatterjee2025}.
L000460	Finally, we demonstrate that Gaussianity can remove the separation between axiomatically and operationally defined sets by proving that Gaussian thermal operations are identical to Gaussian enhanced thermal operations.
L000461	Such closure of the gap between thermal and enhanced thermal operations has not been observed at the channel level in other settings including those allowing catalytic channels~\cite{Son2024RC}.
L000462	
L000463	What are the reasons behind these contrasts?
L000464	We outline a few possible explanations.
L000465	Given that Gaussian thermal operations are Markovian~\cite{Serafini20}, one might ask whether Markovianity is the decisive factor.
L000466	Ref.~\cite{Haagerup2011} gives a negative answer by investigating the Markovian versions of thermal operations and enhanced thermal operations and explicitly constructing a counterexample showing the gap between the two.
L000467	This is a clear sign that other aspects of Gaussianity are more crucial.
L000468	Another physical intuition is that Gaussian operations, generated by quadratic Hamiltonian, decompose into two-mode interactions, unlike general symmetric operations~\cite{Marvian22Locality}.
L000469	This locality might underlie the absence of asymptotic/catalytic advantages and the absence of separation between GEnTO and GTO.
L000470	Further work on the complete characterization of asymptotic and catalytic transformations beyond our single‑mode and second‑moment results would help test this intuition.
L000471	
L000472	We finally point out two promising avenues for future work.
L000473	First is the calculation of approximate implementation cost of GCOs.
L000474	We established that the exact implementation cost diverges for some GCOs including amplifiers, yet they are used in practice.
L000475	It means that with sufficiently large cost, GCOs without free dilation can be approximated well.
L000476	Determining the trade-off between approximation accuracy and implementation cost would impact theorists and practitioners alike.
L000477	Second is the better understanding of $\Sl_{\pm}$ monotones.
L000478	Definition~\ref{def: Sl monotones} suggests that a single mode, corresponding to the vector achieving the optimal ratio of $\chi$ and $\mu$, determines the monotone values, but it is not clear what this mode is for general multi-mode systems.
L000479	We anticipate that better insight into this optimal mode should illuminate hidden structure in Gaussian states.
