L000552	The completeness of $\Sl_{\pm}$ monotones also implies that correlated catalysts (however big and complicated they are) cannot offer any advantage for single-mode transformations.
L000553	\begin{theorem}[No-catalysis]\label{thm: no catalysis}
L000554		Let $S$ be a single-mode system and $C$ be an $m$-mode catalyst system.
L000555	    Suppose that the second moment of $S$ can be transformed as $(\mu_S,\chi_S)\to(\mu_S',\chi_S')$ with the help of a catalytic auxiliary state.
L000556	    In other words, the transformation is possible via a joint GCO on $SC$ that preserves the second moment of the system $C$.
L000557	    Then, there is a GCO acting locally on $S$ achieving $(\mu_S,\chi_S)\toGCO(\mu_S',\chi_S')$.
L000558	\end{theorem}
L000559	\begin{proof}
L000560		The monotonicity of $\Sl_{\pm}$ under correlated catalytic transformation (P4) combined with the completeness of these functions (Lemma~\ref{le:single_mode_state}) implies the theorem.
L000561	\end{proof}
